  "step_2": "The individual reports that the probability in Lottery A appeared more attractive than in B, and that in B the probabilities were 'too close together'. This suggests the individual focused on the probability values themselves, particularly finding the 90% chance in A appealing. The justification centers on the perceived attractiveness of the high probability in A and a discomfort with the similarity of the two probabilities in B. There is no mention of outcomes relative to a reference point, nor of an aspiration level derived from outcomes. The reasoning appears to emphasize probability salience or psychological appeal of high probability, rather than outcome comparisons to a calculated aspiration level.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason requires calculating an aspiration level from outcomes and comparing the probability of exceeding it. The individual does not reference any such calculation, nor do they discuss outcomes in relation to a reference point. Instead, their focus is on the subjective appeal of the probability in A and the proximity of probabilities in B. The information considered (probability magnitudes and their similarity) does not align with the information required by the reason (outcomes relative to an aspiration level). The justification (attractiveness of high probability) also differs from the reason’s rule (prefer lottery with higher chance of exceeding aspiration). Therefore, there is no evidence the individual used the aspiration-level reason.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
