{
  "step_1": "The decision reason is based on the aspiration level, which is calculated as the mean of all outcomes in the decision problem, ignoring their probabilities. The outcomes in the problem are 6000, 0 (from Lottery B) and 3000, 0 (from Lottery A). The unique outcomes are 6000, 3000, and 0. The mean (aspiration level) is (6000 + 3000 + 0) / 3 = 3000. Next, for each lottery, we evaluate the probability that its outcomes exceed this aspiration level (i.e., positive differences from 3000). For Lottery A: 3000 is not greater than 3000 (no positive difference), and 0 is less than 3000. So, the probability of a positive difference is 0%. For Lottery B: 6000 > 3000, which occurs with 0.1% probability; 0 < 3000. So, the probability of a positive difference is 0.1%. Therefore, the decision reason prefers Lottery B, as it has a higher probability (0.1% vs. 0%) of yielding an outcome above the aspiration level.",
