  "step_3": "The decision reason requires calculating an aspiration level (3000) and comparing the probabilities of exceeding it. Only Lottery B (6000 with 0.1%) can exceed this level, so the reason would favor B. However, the individual appears to favor Lottery A, citing its higher probability (0.2% vs 0.1%) of a non-zero outcome, interpreting this as '0.1% more' and 'better probability allocation.' There is no mention of an aspiration level, no reference to evaluating outcomes relative to a benchmark, and no indication that the individual assessed whether outcomes exceed a calculated mean. The focus is purely on the probability of winning, not on deviations from an aspiration level. Therefore, the information considered (probabilities of non-zero outcomes) and the justification (higher chance to win) do not align with the aspiration-level-based reason. The individual’s reasoning is more consistent with a 'maximize chance of winning' heuristic, not the aspiration-level reason.",
  "final_assessment_score": 10
}

{
