  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on whether outcomes exceed an aspiration level (-2333.33), with a preference for lotteries that have a higher chance of such positive deviations. In Lottery B, only the 0 Euro outcome exceeds the aspiration level (positive difference), which occurs with 20% probability. In Lottery A, the outcome (-3000) is below the aspiration level, so no positive difference occurs. Thus, the reason favors B due to its 20% chance of a positive difference. The individual, in the verbal report, explicitly values the 'small chance of not losing anything' — i.e., getting 0 Euros — which aligns with the positive deviation in the decision reason. Although the individual does not mention the aspiration level explicitly, the reasoning hinges on the qualitative distinction between losing (any amount) and not losing, which maps onto the dichotomy of negative vs. positive deviations from a reference point near -2333.33. The focus on the chance of a favorable deviation (avoiding loss) and the dismissal of the certain loss in A matches both the information use and the rule of the decision reason. Therefore, the individual’s reasoning is consistent with the structure of the aspiration-level-based decision rule.",
  "final_assessment_score": 90
